## 1. Finite induction for complex activities

Write gamma incompatible with delta as gamma ~ delta. A compatible subset means that its **distinct** members are mutually compatible. Reflexivity of ~ supplies the exclusion of repeated occurrences and the self term in the budget; it does not prohibit a singleton configuration. This standard hard-core convention is necessary for the recursion and is the convention used by the targets.

Let P be finite, with fixed finite nonnegative rho and a, and set mu_gamma = rho_gamma exp(a_gamma). Assume sum_(delta ~ gamma) mu_delta <= a_gamma for every gamma. The budget is imposed on P, so it also bounds every restricted gas without changing any majorant.

Induct simultaneously over all allowed sets Gamma of a given size and all gamma in Gamma. The empty gas is one. Put W = Gamma minus {gamma} and N = {delta in W: delta ~ gamma}. Compatible configurations either omit gamma or contain it and omit every member of N. Thus

    Z_Gamma = Z_W + w_gamma Z_(W minus N).

Both partition functions on the right have fewer allowed types than Gamma. In particular Z_W is nonzero by induction, before it is used as a denominator. List the members of N and remove them successively from W. Each individual removal ratio belongs to a gas of size at most |W|, so invoking the induction at every stage is legitimate; no ratio of the current unknown-size gas is used to prove itself. If H contains delta, the induction gives

    |Z_H / Z_(H minus {delta})| >= exp(-mu_delta).

Multiplying the reciprocals of these lower bounds gives

    |Z_(W minus N) / Z_W| <= exp(sum_(delta in N) mu_delta).

Self-incompatibility is decisive: gamma belongs to the full budget but not to N, so the last exponent is at most a_gamma - mu_gamma. Hence, for q = Z_Gamma/Z_W - 1,

    |q| <= rho_gamma exp(a_gamma-mu_gamma)
         = mu_gamma exp(-mu_gamma)
         <= 1-exp(-mu_gamma) < 1.

The scalar inequality is exp(mu) >= 1+mu for mu >= 0. If mu_gamma = 0, rho_gamma = 0 and q = 0 exactly; there is no division by mu. The disk around one has a strictly positive margin exp(-mu_gamma) from zero, including when the budget is attained with equality. Thus Z_Gamma is nonzero, and the desired lower bound on its deletion ratio is obtained for the next induction step.

The scalar logarithm series is valid because |q| < 1, and it is the principal logarithm of 1+q:

    |Log(1+q)| <= sum_(k>=1) |q|^k/k
                = -log(1-|q|) <= mu_gamma.

This proves FINITE-LOG1603 lines 7–34 for all complex phases, not merely for positive or real weights. It does not use a connected expansion, positivity of the original factors, a lower bound on the total normalizer independent of volume, or a majorant selected afresh at each activity value.

If the self term were omitted, a one-type gas with a = 0, rho = 1 and w = -1 would satisfy the empty-neighbor budget but have Z = 0. The target identifies precisely this obstruction. Conversely, inserting the self term again as a loop in the simple graph used for the external theorem would change that graph's independent-set interpretation and is not the intended translation.

## 2. Analytic branch, boundary and deletion costs

For fixed finite P, each Z_H is a polynomial. The induction gives nonvanishing for every H and every point of the fixed closed polydisc. Each deletion ratio is consequently holomorphic in its interior and remains in the open right half-plane, where its principal logarithm is single-valued. For any ordering of the allowed types, sum the corresponding logarithms along a deletion chain to the empty gas. The resulting L_Gamma is holomorphic, vanishes at zero, and satisfies exp(L_Gamma) = Z_Gamma.

Two deletion orders give logarithms whose difference is continuous, takes values in 2 pi i times the integers, and is zero at the origin. On the connected polydisc their difference is therefore zero. The same reasoning shows that the difference L_Gamma - L_(Gamma minus X) equals the sum of principal deletion-ratio logarithms along any chain removing Gamma intersect X. The triangle inequality gives

    |L_Gamma - L_(Gamma minus X)| <= sum_(gamma in Gamma intersect X) mu_gamma.

In particular, |L_Gamma| <= sum_(gamma in Gamma) mu_gamma, and taking real parts gives the claimed upper and lower modulus bounds for Z_Gamma. There is no branch ambiguity in this argument, but **L_Gamma need not be the principal logarithm of Z_Gamma**. FINITE-LOG1603 correctly uses principal Log for the ratios and a normalized analytic log for the total gas.

For example, take 32 mutually compatible distinct types, with self incompatibility, a = log 2, rho = 1/8 and w = i/8. Then mu = 1/4 <= log 2, and the normalized total logarithm is 32 Log(1+i/8). Its imaginary part is 32 arctan(1/8), between 191/48 and 4. These bounds lie strictly between pi and 2 pi, so the principal logarithm of the product differs by 2 pi i. This is a warning against enlarging the target's branch claim, not a counterexample to it.

All ratio bounds already hold on the closed polydisc. Continuity gives the closed-domain logarithm inequalities as well. More explicitly, compactness and finiteness give a neighborhood on which the finitely many denominator polynomials remain nonzero and the ratio images avoid the logarithm cut. Thus equality in the majorant budget causes no boundary zero. If some rho_gamma vanish, freeze those activities at zero and interpret the polydisc interior in the remaining coordinates. The polynomial/logarithm also has a local extension in the omitted coordinates; an empty full-dimensional interior is not an obstruction.

One wording precision is useful when comparing the two targets: for a **fixed finite gas**, holomorphy on a neighborhood of its closed polydisc already gives qualitative absolute Taylor convergence there. FINITE-LOG1603's reservation about Mayer coefficient bounds should be read as not supplying the identified, quantitative pinned-cluster bound with the stated uniform constants. DOBRUSHIN-DICTIONARY1604 supplies that additional statement through the selected theorem. No false finite analyticity assertion is needed for this distinction.

## 3. Exact activity and support dictionary

Let I be the declared finite original label universe, with nonempty raw-coordinate supports D_i. For a nonempty label subset S put U(S) = union_(i in S) D_i. The polymer types are these nonempty original subsets, with the exact connected forest activities z(S) defined in COMPONENT-ALGEBRA1574. They are not arbitrary cumulants of merged observables. A type need not be connected in the raw support-overlap graph: covariance links in its defining forest may connect its distinct overlap components.

The supplied finite forest identity and its validity for the fixed factors are premises here, not newly audited external proofs. Given that identity, the moment-level regrouping has the following required dictionary. Expansion of product_(i in I)(1+f_i) selects each original label at most once. Its moment expansion partitions selected labels into blocks whose raw supports are disjoint. These blocks form a compatible family of the declared polymer types, and conversely such a family has a unique selected union. Because every D_i is nonempty, disjoint raw supports also prevent reuse of an original label across different types in a family. Hence the resulting gas has

    S ~ T  if and only if  U(S) intersects U(T).

This includes S ~ S. Disjoint **label sets** alone would not be a sufficient compatibility rule: two different original labels can share a scalar coordinate. The targets use raw supports correctly. The gas weights can be complex or signed; original cutoff-tail signs require no change in the finite induction.

Let the original label-overlap graph have maximum degree Delta. For a test label set T define C(T) as the union of the closed label-neighborhoods of its members, including each member itself. Then |C(T)| <= (Delta+1)|T|, and

    U(S) intersects U(T)  if and only if  S intersects C(T).

For mu_S = |z(S)| exp(eta |S|), nonnegative summation therefore gives

    sum_(S ~ T) mu_S
      <= sum_(i in C(T)) sum_(S contains i) mu_S
      <= (Delta+1)|T| b_eta.

This is the exact incompatibility union bound needed in both targets. It counts the self term, handles overlaps between distinct labels and tolerates multiple counting. It introduces no component N!, no new ordering of original factors, and no Mayer-family factorial.

The 1592 rooted estimate is used as the supplied premise

    sup_i sum_(S contains i) |z(S)| exp(eta |S|) <= b_eta,
    b_eta = lambda_eta [1 - A log(1-lambda_eta A R)],
    lambda_eta = lambda exp(eta),
    A = e sqrt(6),  R = max(1, Delta+2BDmJ).

Its hypotheses, including the exact all-subsets product norm, bounded original supports and combined incidence, and the uniform original covariance row sum, are not discharged by invoking this formula. This assessment does not replace that premise by the previous report's conclusion or re-review its two old targets.

## 4. Independent selected-source reading and Dobrushin translation

I accessed the authorized [author-hosted Cluster Expansion exposition](https://www.cts.cuni.cz/~kotecky/publ/K-CE-EMP06.pdf) directly in memory. Both responses had HTTP 200, 208,045 bytes and SHA256 `2a56f2e4a3285aedf7934c6aa235e3da0dca3131b5ce52f4076e8c94819c6e5a`; the PDF has eleven pages. The first attempt obtained the correct bytes but failed to display a ligature through the local character encoding. Its failure metadata is retained. A second request with UTF-8 output successfully exposed printed pages 3–5, which I read. Neither the PDF nor extracted source body/excerpts were written to disk. The retained files contain only my helper, metadata and paraphrase.

The selected pages define the finite partition function on independent vertex sets, Taylor multiindices and the connected repeated-vertex graph; state condition 24; and state the cluster expansion theorem (i)–(ii). In that simple-graph formulation, neighbors are other incompatible types. Under radii bounded by r_v times the product of (1-r_u) over these neighbors, with 0 <= r_v < 1, the theorem gives finite nonvanishing, an absolutely convergent logarithmic expansion on the closed polydisc, and a sum over coefficients whose support contains v bounded by -log(1-r_v). These are the precise selected conclusions used here. I did not audit the whole external proof, independently check the publication-list bibliography, or access the original 1986 article.

The targets' self-including budget uses the same independent-set partition polynomial after suppressing the reflexive diagonal when constructing the simple graph. Set r_S = 1-exp(-mu_S). For the neighbor set N(S) excluding S,

    rho_S exp(sum_(T in N(S)) mu_T)
      <= rho_S exp(a_S-mu_S)
       = mu_S exp(-mu_S)
      <= 1-exp(-mu_S) = r_S.

Multiplication by exp(-sum_(T in N(S)) mu_T) gives exactly

    rho_S <= r_S product_(T in N(S)) (1-r_T).

This verifies DOBRUSHIN-DICTIONARY1604 lines 13–22. The self term was used once to subtract mu_S. It is neither lost nor included a second time in the neighbor product. Zero mu_S causes no singularity: r_S = rho_S = 0, as permitted by the selected statement. The pinned bound is -log(1-r_S) = mu_S exactly.

The selected statement, rather than an assumed reading of the 1986 theorem, is the external premise for the coefficient conclusion. The new successful reading does not erase the historical failed requests recorded in the frozen packet. The finite proof in Section 1 stands on its own and does not depend on success of this fetch.

## 5. Multiindices, roots and the two size budgets

The partition polynomial contains each type at most once in each compatible family, but its logarithm has multiindices X and may repeat a type. For one isolated type, log(1+w) already has coefficients (-1)^(k+1)/k for every k >= 1. In the selected source's coefficient graph, k occurrences of one type form a complete graph; distinct occurrences of two incompatible types are also connected. The multiindex coefficient contains its prescribed product of multiplicity factorials. This is unrelated to the original-set ordering factor in the 1592 activity estimate or to an unearned component factorial.

The selected pinned estimate sums each cluster term once when S belongs to supp X. It has **no X(S) multiplier**. For an original label i, the indicator that at least one type occurring in X contains i is bounded by the sum of these type-presence indicators over S containing i. Thus

    sum_(X containing original label i) |a(X)| |z|^X
      <= sum_(S contains i) sum_(X: S in supp X) |a(X)| |z|^X
      <= sum_(S contains i) mu_S <= b_eta.

This proves the root interpretation in lines 34–36. A term may be overcounted because several distinct types contain i, which only increases the nonnegative upper bound. Repeated occurrences of one such type do not create another factor X(S) in this argument. No original-label derivative, physical field derivative, counterterm coefficient or normalized marginal identity is thereby identified.

To require the extra cluster weight exp(theta L(X)), where L(X) = sum_S X(S)|S| and theta >= 0, replace the activity radii by rho'_S = |z(S)| exp(theta |S|). Then

    product_S (rho'_S)^X(S)
      = |z|^X exp(theta sum_S X(S)|S|),
    mu'_S = rho'_S exp(eta |S|)
           = |z(S)| exp((eta+theta)|S|).

The criterion budget remains a_S = eta |S|. Consequently the rooted bound at eta+theta must satisfy

    (Delta+1)b_(eta+theta) <= eta,
    lambda exp(eta+theta) A R < 1.

This is exactly the separation in lines 38–48. Replacing the first right-hand side by eta+theta does not establish the intended eta-budget at the inflated radii. As a simple abstract warning, a single type with eta = log 2, theta = log 8 and rho = 1/8 has mu' = 2 <= log 16 = eta+theta, but its inflated radius is one and includes the zero at w = -1. The correct eta-budget fails. This example concerns the abstract misbudget; it is not claimed to satisfy the separate b-convergence prerequisite or to contradict the correctly stated two-budget result.

Repeated-type size is L(X), not the cardinality of a union with repetitions removed. For nonnegative theta a bound using L(X) can dominate a smaller union-size weight, but these quantities are not equal. Neither by itself supplies physical spatial decay: that requires an appropriate covariance/geometry estimate with its own constants and budget.

For fixed factors, inserting auxiliary scalar intensities u_i into product_i(1+u_i f_i) multiplies each forest activity by product_(i in S)u_i. Thus the polydisc |u_i| <= u0 is controlled by replacing lambda with u0 lambda. This uses the same factors, covariance and raw supports; it is a polynomial reweighting of original labels. It supplies no analytic admission for moving cutoffs, complex background/current variables, or physical covariances that change with those parameters.

## 6. Smallness, extensive costs and retained source objections

For t = lambda exp(eta), if t A R <= 1/2 then -log(1-t A R) <= log 2, so b_eta <= t(1+A log 2). The two explicit inequalities in FINITE-LOG1603 lines 59–60 therefore imply both convergence of the supplied rooted majorant and (Delta+1)b_eta <= eta. Replacing t with lambda exp(eta+theta), while keeping eta in the numerator of the second inequality, proves the window in DOBRUSHIN-DICTIONARY1604 lines 45–46. The inequalities can be non-strict as written because the first keeps the convergence argument at most one half. They are sufficient, not necessary, conditions; failure of this window alone is not a divergence proof.

Uniformity requires the actual B,D,m,Delta,J and mixed-factor constants to be uniform in the intended family. In particular, m must count interaction and original cutoff-tail labels together. A lower spectral gap and upper operator norm do not imply an absolute row-sum bound J: a symmetric Hadamard example C_n = I + epsilon H_n/sqrt(n), with fixed 0 < epsilon < 1, has uniform eigenvalue bounds but off-diagonal absolute row sums epsilon(n-1)/sqrt(n). The retained row-sum objection is not removed by the new logarithm theorem. Nor does smallness of an insertion/tail parameter alone establish the required joint inequalities if other constants grow with coupling, scale or background.

Summing roots gives

    sum_S mu_S <= sum_S |S| mu_S
                 = sum_(i in I) sum_(S contains i) mu_S
                 <= |I| b_eta.

Consequently |L| <= |I|b_eta and |1/Z| <= exp(|I|b_eta). This is a real finite lower bound on |Z| under the hypotheses, but it is not uniform in |I|. Independent types with weight -1/8 satisfy the self-budget for a = log 2 and have Z = (7/8)^M, so their reciprocal grows as (8/7)^M. No theorem of this form can erase that example. It is also compatible with the earlier independent-cutoff warning about the original unnormalized law.

If X consists of the types incompatible with an original test-label set A0, the same closed-neighborhood union bound gives sum_(S in X) mu_S <= (Delta+1)|A0|b_eta. Combining this with the telescoping inequality proves the stated gas-deletion logarithm cost. Removing abstract polymer types is not conditioning a physical measure, changing a source cutoff, or proving conditional independence.

The following limitations remain in force:

- The actual finite source law, selected/free carriers, maximal-domain/variational geometry and actual raw supports have not been inspected or identified here.
- The exact fixed-factor forest representation and the old real-Gaussian mixed-product argument are supplied premises. No external forest proof or formal import/build was audited.
- Combined label multiplicity, uniform absolute J, the all-selected-subsets norm and an actual scale/coupling/background smallness regime remain to be established for the source.
- The old adverse component-placement witness and rejected component majorant are not validated by this new gas criterion. The old conditional original-label repair is context, not a freshly reassessed target.
- The earlier nonsymmetric tagged inverse, exterior-support reference mismatch, moving/complex-domain and original-cutoff objections are not repaired by this finite gas theorem. Their underlying source artifacts are absent; later claimed source repairs have not been checked here.
- Any inherited interaction estimate still requires its nonnegative insertion range and original product/tail assumptions; the present criterion cannot substitute positivity of the gas for those hypotheses.
- Correct baseline allocation, physical normalization, normalized marginal transport and intrinsic counterterm identification remain open. Neither finite zero-freeness nor a local abstract cluster sum establishes them.
- No infinite-volume physical construction, spatial mass gap, continuum result, novelty claim, manuscript acceptance or journal peer review is supplied.

The new conditional deduction does close the particular **abstract finite zero-free/logarithm step**, and the selected classical theorem closes the stated **abstract coefficient step**. Those are narrower than the unresolved physical normalization and source-admission obligations.

## 7. Independent finite verification

The new helper `review-tmp/independent-check1604.py` uses Python 3.12.6 and exact integer/fraction arithmetic. It imports nothing from the supplied checker and makes no network requests. Its retained result records every test family, the adverse examples, and the scope limits.

1. All 64 simple incompatibility graphs on four distinct types were tested with 20 complex phase patterns each. Fixed rational radii and a = log 2 satisfy the self-including budgets through the rational certificate sum mu <= 1/2 <= log 2. Direct independent-set sums produced 20,480 nonzero partition values. They were compared against 40,960 exact deletion recursions and rational disk inequalities. The partition values were not generated by the recursion being tested.
2. On the same 64 graphs, formal multiplication of the partition polynomial and the truncated scalar log series was compared with direct enumeration of connected spanning subgraphs of repeated-type graphs. All 4,416 multiindex coefficients of total degree one through four agreed, including repeated types, disconnected-support zeros and the alternating sign. There were 256 pinned partial-sum checks against mu. Finite partial sums do not prove convergence or the full theorem (ii).
3. An additional rational Dobrushin radius r = mu/(1+mu) was checked for each type in those examples, giving 256 exact criterion checks. This is a finite corroborating certificate, not a substitution for the target's analytically proved r = 1-exp(-mu) dictionary.
4. A four-original-label support universe, with 15 polymer types and Delta = 2, verified every support-neighborhood equivalence and the self-including union bound. It includes different labels with overlapping raw supports, so confusing label disjointness with support disjointness would fail this check.
5. The retained examples exhibit the one-type zero after omitting self incompatibility, a total logarithm whose normalized branch differs from the principal logarithm, the singleton repeated-type coefficients, and extensive reciprocals. The eta/theta warning is kept with its narrower scope explicitly stated.

These tests are finite corroboration. The arbitrary-size deletion proof is given in Section 1, and the full absolute-cluster conclusion rests on the selected external theorem as stated. No test verifies a physical source hypothesis or machine-checks either universal theorem.

## Disposition

Both exact new targets are supported within their declared conditional scope. FINITE-LOG1603 establishes finite complex zero-freeness and normalized logarithm/deletion bounds with the extensive cost retained. DOBRUSHIN-DICTIONARY1604 correctly converts the same budget to the independently read selected Dobrushin criterion and obtains the stated absolute cluster and original-root bounds, with repeated types and eta/theta costs treated correctly. All source-admission, uniform-input, physical-normalization and external-proof limits above remain open. No grade or broader assessment is issued.
